# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-4]  With the  [LINK: (Pacification of Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacification_of_Ghent]  on 8 November 1576, the States General of the  [LINK: (Seventeen Provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seventeen_Provinces] , except  [LINK: (Luxemburg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Luxemburg] , managed to articulate a joint Catholic-Protestant political and military rebellion against the Spanish imperial government. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTEvan_der_Lem1995Chapter_IV-1]  But various political, religious and military circumstances caused this union to collapse in 1579, the year in which the Netherlands was divided in two, with the Catholic provinces of the south joining in the  [LINK: (Union of Arras) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_of_Arras]  on 6 January and the Protestant provinces of the north (in general terms) at the  [LINK: (Union of Utrecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_of_Utrecht]  of 23 January. The southern provinces would once again be under the orbit of the Spanish government, while the northern provinces would reaffirm their political and military alliance against Spain. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMarek_y_Villarino_de_Brugge2020bv.&nbsp;II_pp.&nbsp;95–124-2]  [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTEvan_der_Lem1995Chapter_V-3]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-5]  The rebellious provinces of the Netherlands managed to form a joint political and military rebellion against Spain after the  [LINK: (Pacification of Ghent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacification_of_Ghent]  on 8 November 1576. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTEvan_der_Lem1995Chapter_IV-1]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-8]  In 1576, the States General called, at the suggestion of William the Silent,  [LINK: (Francis, Duke of Anjou) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis,_Duke_of_Anjou] , to request his protection. In 1578 Anjou intervened with an army of French in the south of the Netherlands, but did not achieve the expected results and withdrew. In the following years he again invaded the southern Netherlands, and on 23 January 1581 the  [LINK: (Treaty of Plessis-les-Tours) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Plessis-les-Tours]  was ratified between Anjou and the States General to agree to his reign in the region. His intervention in the Netherlands ended in 1583 after several defeats of his forces. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-6]  [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-7]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-11]  After the outbreak of the Portuguese rebellion in 1640, on 12 June 1641, to the detriment of Spain, a truce and alliance agreement was reached between the Dutch Republic and Portugal with the  [LINK: (Treaty of The Hague) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_The_Hague_(1641)] . [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-9]  But this agreement was only limited to Europe, thus continuing the struggle between the Dutch and Portuguese in the colonies. [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-Tena-10]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-13]  The nascent political organization reached by the rebellious northern provinces with the  [LINK: (Union of Utrecht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_of_Utrecht]  on 23 January 1579,  [LINK: (Groenveld 2009) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#CITEREFGroenveld2009] , pp. 16–17  [LINK: (Groenveld 2009) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#CITEREFGroenveld2009] , pp. 10–11 would be followed by the  [LINK: (Act of Abjuration) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Abjuration]  on 26 July 1581, declaring de facto independence from Spain,  [LINK: (Groenveld 2009) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#CITEREFGroenveld2009] , pp. 18–19 to finally become a republic by approving the  [LINK: (Instruction of 12 April 1588) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instruction_of_12_April_1588] . [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGroenveld200921-12]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-15]  During the  [LINK: (Thirty Years' War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thirty_Years%27_War] , the Holy Roman Empire supported Spain with Imperial forces on the Low Countries front in 1629, 1632, and 1635, although it never directly waged war against the Dutch Republic. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-14]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-19]   [LINK: (Dutch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_language] : Tachtigjarige Oorlog;  [LINK: (Spanish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanish_language] : Guerra de los Ochenta Años or Guerra de Flandes, lit. 'War of Flanders'

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-20]  Dutch: Nederlandse Opstand

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-21]  There is disagreement about name and periodisation of the war, see  [LINK: (Historiography of the Eighty Years' War § Name and periodisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historiography_of_the_Eighty_Years%27_War#Name_and_periodisation] .

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-22]  The  [LINK: (Habsburg Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habsburg_Netherlands]  were at the time also known as the  [LINK: (Seventeen Provinces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seventeen_Provinces] , today roughly covering the  [LINK: (Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands] ,  [LINK: (Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium] ,  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg]  and parts of northern France, but excluding areas such as the  [LINK: (Principality of Liège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Li%C3%A8ge] .

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-24]  Constitutionally, the  [LINK: (Crowns of Castile) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_of_Castile]  and  [LINK: (Aragon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_of_Aragon]  would not be united into the Kingdom of Spain until the 1707–1716  [LINK: (Nueva Planta decrees) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nueva_Planta_decrees] , and Charles formally reigned as Charles I of Castile and Aragon (sometimes informally called "Spain"). But in historiography, he is more commonly known as Emperor Charles V.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-25]  Unless otherwise indicated, "Netherlands" and "Netherlandish" refer here to the entire area of the Habsburg Netherlands and its inhabitants (including modern  [LINK: (Belgium) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Belgium] ,  [LINK: (Luxembourg) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxembourg]  and parts of northern France, but excluding areas such as the  [LINK: (Principality of Liège) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Li%C3%A8ge] ), whereas "Dutch Republic" and "Dutch" will refer to the country, currently known as the  [LINK: (Netherlands) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netherlands] , and its inhabitants.

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-28]  "...the starting phase of the Revolt in Zeeland. We label the 1566–1572 period as the strike up to the Revolt: years in which the resistance against central authority, grown out to a rebellion, began to powerfully manifest itself." [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-FOOTNOTERooze-Stouthamer200911–12-27]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_ref-Maanen_62-0]  The Dutch States General, for dramatic effect, decided to promulgate the ratification of the Peace of Münster (which was actually ratified by them on 15 May 1648) on the 80th anniversary of the execution of the Counts of Egmont and Horne, 5 June 1648. [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighty_Years%27_War#cite_note-61]


